    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_69a45c15290b46ec319388fc26c5d338e84172a02a964d8f947f42a3feb67610",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The damping that makes the normalized sequence look like a clean first-zero sinusoid also limits what that appearance can test. Exponentially decreasing mode coefficients make the first critical-line zero dominate the log-periodic wave and let sign-change ratios recover its frequency, while the same suppression can delay the contribution of a hypothetical high off-line zero until an enormous index. Thus frequency recovery and finite containment of k^{3/4}c_k test the dominant-mode model but have weak reach against remote spectral defects.",
